-- thick guide: us-mocra-cosmetics-facility-registration (1703 words body, 46 words short answer)

UPDATE public.guides SET body = $thickbodyD$# US MoCRA: facility registration and product listing for cosmetics

The Modernization of Cosmetics Regulation Act of 2022 (MoCRA) amended the Federal Food, Drug, and Cosmetic Act to create mandatory facility registration and product listing for cosmetics in the United States. Under section 607 of the FD&C Act, owners or operators of facilities that manufacture or process cosmetic products must register with the FDA and renew every two years, and the responsible person for each marketed cosmetic product must list it with the FDA, including its ingredients, with annual updates. This guide explains the duties for brands selling cosmetics in the US, including foreign brands.

## Key facts

- MoCRA was enacted in December 2022 as part of omnibus legislation, amending the FD&C Act; before it, FDA cosmetics registration was voluntary.
- Every person owning or operating a facility engaged in manufacturing or processing a cosmetic product for US distribution must register each facility and renew the registration every two years.
- The responsible person for each marketed cosmetic product must submit a cosmetic product listing, including ingredients, with annual updates.
- The responsible person is defined in section 604(4): the manufacturer, packer or distributor whose name appears on the product label.
- Responsible persons must report serious adverse events to the FDA within 15 business days and hold safety substantiation records.
- Registration and listing are not FDA approval: the FDA provides no certificates verifying compliance, and marketing must never imply endorsement.
- Certain small businesses are exempt from registration and listing under section 612, but the exemption is narrow.

## What MoCRA changed

Before MoCRA, FDA cosmetics registration was voluntary. MoCRA made it mandatory: every person owning or operating a facility engaged in manufacturing or processing a cosmetic product for US distribution must register each facility, and the responsible person must submit a cosmetic product listing for each marketed product. The FDA's guidance for industry on registration and listing sets out the data elements, the electronic submission portal, and answers to common questions such as who qualifies as a responsible person. Certain small businesses are exempt from the registration and listing requirements under section 612, but the exemption is narrow and should be checked against the statute. The FDA exercised enforcement discretion during the transition, with mandatory compliance expected from 1 July 2024; confirm current enforcement posture in FDA communications before planning around any grace period.

## Facility registration

Each facility that manufactures or processes cosmetic products for distribution in the US must be registered, including contract manufacturers working for your brand. Registration is submitted electronically through the FDA's portal and must be renewed every two years; calendar the renewal as a compliance obligation, not an administrative nicety. The registration identifies the facility, its activities and its contact details, and the FDA assigns a facility registration number. That number is an administrative identifier: it does not indicate FDA approval of the facility or its products. Foreign facilities register in the same system; the practical challenge is ensuring the registration data stays current as contract manufacturing arrangements change.

## Product listing

The responsible person must submit a cosmetic product listing for each marketed cosmetic product, including its ingredients, with annual updates. The listing identifies the product, the responsible person, the facility where it is manufactured or processed, and the ingredient list. Annual updates keep the listing current as formulations, ingredients or responsible-person details change; discontinued products should be removed from active listing. Because the listing ties products to facilities and responsible persons, private-label arrangements need clear allocation: the contract should state who lists each product and who updates the listing when the formulation changes.

## The responsible person

The responsible person is defined in section 604(4) of the FD&C Act: the manufacturer, packer or distributor whose name appears on the product label. A company outside the US can be the responsible person if it meets that definition, though the label must carry domestic contact information through which adverse event reports can be received. For example, a Korean skincare brand selling in the US typically designates a US agent or distributor structure so that FDA communications and adverse event reporting have a clear owner. The responsible person carries the listing, adverse event reporting and record-keeping duties, so designate the role deliberately in writing rather than letting it default to whoever's name is on the label.

## Adverse events and records

Responsible persons must report serious adverse events to the FDA within 15 business days of receiving them, and must hold records supporting the safety substantiation of their products. Safety substantiation means having adequate evidence that the product is safe under its labelled conditions of use; the FDA can request these records. Build an intake workflow: a channel for receiving reports (including the domestic contact on the label), triage criteria for what counts as serious, a 15-business-day clock with escalation, and record retention that keeps adverse event records available for FDA review. MoCRA requires adverse event records to be held for six years.

## Good manufacturing practice and facility suspension

MoCRA directs the FDA to establish good manufacturing practice regulations for cosmetics, and gives the FDA authority to suspend a facility's registration where there is a reasonable probability that a cosmetic manufactured or processed there causes serious adverse health consequences. The GMP rulemaking sets expectations for manufacturing controls that brands should anticipate in supplier qualification; the suspension authority makes facility compliance a business-continuity issue, not just a regulatory one. Monitor the GMP rulemaking and align contract manufacturers early.

## No FDA certificates

A practical point the FDA has stressed: cosmetic product facility registration and product listing is neither an approval programme nor a promotional tool. The assignment of a facility registration number or product listing number does not indicate FDA approval. The FDA does not provide certificates or other documents to verify compliance with registration, and businesses should be wary of anyone selling such certificates, including demands seen on e-commerce platforms. Never imply FDA endorsement in marketing, on packaging or in marketplace listings; it invites enforcement and erodes trust.

## Duty allocation table

| Actor | Registration | Product listing | Adverse events | Records |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Brand owning the formula (responsible person) | Ensures its facilities are registered | Lists each product, annual updates | Reports within 15 business days | Safety substantiation, 6-year adverse event records |
| Contract manufacturer | Registers each facility, biennial renewal | Supports listing with facility data | Forwards reports to the responsible person | Manufacturing records per GMP expectations |
| Foreign brand | Registers via its structure; US contact on label | Lists products it is responsible person for | Needs a US intake point | Same as domestic responsible person |
| Distributor (name on label) | May be the responsible person | Lists if it is the responsible person | Reports if it is the responsible person | As responsible person |

## Compliance checklist

- [ ] Identify every facility manufacturing or processing your US cosmetic products, including contract manufacturers.
- [ ] Register each facility with the FDA and calendar the two-year renewal.
- [ ] Designate the responsible person for each product and confirm the label carries the right contact details.
- [ ] Submit a product listing for each marketed cosmetic product, including ingredients.
- [ ] Set an annual process for listing updates.
- [ ] Build a serious-adverse-event intake and 15-business-day reporting workflow.
- [ ] Hold safety substantiation records in a form the FDA can review, with six-year adverse event retention.
- [ ] Check whether the small-business exemption genuinely applies before relying on it.
- [ ] Allocate registration, listing and reporting duties in every manufacturing and private-label contract.
- [ ] Never market registration or listing as FDA approval, and reject anyone selling compliance certificates.

## What this means for businesses

For businesses, MoCRA turns US cosmetics compliance into an ongoing administrative programme, not a one-time filing. Foreign brands need a clear responsible-person structure and a US contact point for adverse events. Private-label and contract-manufacturing arrangements must allocate who registers the facility and who lists the product, because the duties attach to different actors. However, note that registration is not approval: marketing materials must never imply FDA endorsement, and enforcement attention on incomplete listings is increasing.

## How the US compares with the EU

The **United States** requires facility registration and product listing through the FDA under MoCRA, with a responsible person and adverse event reporting. The **EU** requires pre-market notification through the CPNP portal under the Cosmetics Regulation, with an EU-based responsible person holding the product information file. The two systems are not mutually exclusive and do not recognise each other: a brand selling in both markets completes both.

## Is facility registration the same as FDA approval?

No, registration and listing are not an approval programme.

## Can a foreign company be the responsible person?

Yes, if it meets the statutory definition, with domestic contact details on the label.

## What about the old voluntary programme?

The voluntary cosmetics registration programme was ended; MoCRA filings do not transfer from it.

## Are there exemptions?

Certain small businesses are exempt under section 612; verify against the statute.

## How often must listings be updated?

Annually, and whenever the underlying information changes. Calendar the annual update as a recurring obligation.

## What records must be kept?

Safety substantiation records supporting that products are safe under labelled conditions of use, and adverse event records held for six years, in a form the FDA can review.

## Assumptions and limitations

- This guide summarises the registration, listing and reporting framework; labelling, good manufacturing practice and ingredient rules are separate topics.
- FDA guidance and enforcement practice continue to develop; confirm current portal procedures before filing.
- State-level cosmetic rules (for example in California) sit alongside federal requirements.

## Official sources

- [FDA: Modernization of Cosmetics Regulation Act of 2022 (MoCRA)](https://www.fda.gov/cosmetics/cosmetics-laws-regulations/modernization-cosmetics-regulation-act-2022-mocra)
- [FDA: no certificates for cosmetic facility registration](https://www.fda.gov/cosmetics/cosmetics-news-events/fda-clarifies-it-does-not-provide-certificates-or-other-documents-verify-compliance-cosmetic-product)
- [FDA guidance for industry: registration and listing of cosmetic product facilities and products](https://www.fda.gov/media/170732/download)

